4. RESTRICTIONS ON DERIVATIVES & PUBLISHING
You may NOT Publish or Distribute any Derivative work unless it meets the
requirement of containing Major Functional Changes.
- Minor Changes (such as changing the name, logo, icons, colors, or visual
  theme; modifying the package name; bug fixes, refactoring, etc.) DO NOT
  grant permission to Publish or Distribute.
- All Derivative works must use a unique, non-confusing name and package
  name to ensure they are not mistaken for the original PocketOps app.
- "Major Functional Changes" require that new features and functional
  modifications constitute at least 30% of the entire codebase and
  functionality of the project.
- Bug fixes, refactoring, and UI/theme adjustments DO NOT count toward the 30%
  threshold. Only entirely new features or substantial functional expansions
  qualify.
- The Author (Aakarsh/L192) serves as the sole and final arbiter in
  determining if the 30% functional change threshold has been met.

5. SHARE-ALIKE AND OPEN SOURCE FORCE (COPYLEFT)
If you meet the 30% Major Functional Changes threshold and choose to Publish
or Distribute a Derivative work, you are legally obligated to:
- Keep the entire source code of your Derivative work fully public and
  open-source. You cannot close the source code, sell it privately, or hide
  your modifications.
- Distribute your Derivative work under this exact same license (or a
  compatible GPL-3.0 license), ensuring that any downstream users have the
  same rights and restrictions.
- Platform Requirement: If the Derivative is hosted on a public repository
  platform (such as GitHub), it must be managed via that platform’s native
  "Fork" mechanism to maintain a transparent, verifiable link to the original
  repository.

6. PROTECTION OF AUTHORSHIP
Any permitted Derivative work must:
- Retain all original copyright notices, credits, and trademarks pointing to
  Aakarsh (L192) / IIXII™.
- Prominently state inside the application (within the "About" or "Settings"
  menu) that it is a "Fork of IIXII™ property" and include a clear link to the
  original PocketOps repository.
- Include a comprehensive changelog within the repository detailing all
  functional modifications.

